An ultra-wide panoramic undersea view spans across the broad sandy shoal, where Salomé — a slender merwoman with long pale silver-blue hair falling past her waist, pale grey-green eyes, wearing a pale shell-bead strand across her shoulders and a silver-blue scaled mermaid tail ending in a wide fan fin — hovers beside the flat-topped bell rock at the centre-right, calling across the open water with her head lifted toward the distant arch. At the far upper-left, blocking the dark silhouette of the coral archway, Mina the moray eel — waist-high in scale with a dark olive-brown body, pale yellow-cream mottling, and a long dorsal fin — keeps her broad mouth clamped firmly shut across the opening, her large round dark-brown eyes glaring with brows drawn low. Sunlit water blankets the vast expanse of pale ochre sand and scattered coral heads.

