In a close-up at the base of the pale coral pillar inside the arch, an arm-sized coral branch — straight, pale cream, with a rough surface, tapering to a point at one end and showing a clean square break at its thick base — lies flat on the smooth ochre sand in the foreground below an empty, squared notch cut into the pillar at upper-centre. In the midground behind the coral pillar, Mina the moray eel — waist-high in scale with a dark olive-brown body and cream mottling — curves her head forward, large round dark-brown eyes glaring with brows pulled down and her broad mouth clamped tightly shut. In the midground, Élodie — an adult woman with light brown wavy mid-back hair parted in the centre, brown eyes, wearing a purple long-sleeve swim top with gathered waist and a shimmering purple scaled mermaid tail ending in a fan-shaped fin with a sheer yellow sash — floats attentively with her brown eyes fixed on Mina.

